# …BUT the ability string pool IS tracked, and the negation must come after the
# `cards/build/*.bin` rule above or git never sees it.
#
# WHY: engine_c_wip compiles RBKA_STRINGS_OFFSETS (the (chunk, start, len)
# offsets into this file) into src/core/generated/gen_data.c, which is COMMITTED.
# The offsets and the bytes they point at are two halves of one artifact: if only
# the offsets are versioned, anyone who runs cards/compile_abilities.py
# regenerates the pool out from under them. The pool is UNSEPARATED, so a few
# bytes of drift is not an error -- it silently returns a byte splice of two
# neighbouring entries ("cost_limit" -> "cost_limitc", "left_side" -> "eft_side",
# "resource_type" -> "ent_orderchoi"). This happened on 2026-09-30 and again on
# 2026-10-01, breaking 494 of 985 abilities.
#
# The blanket `cards/build/*.bin` rule came from a 2026-08-22 repo-hygiene round
# and predates the C port's use of this file; it was never a decision to leave
# the ability offsets unversioned. cards.bin / abilities.bin / abilities.bin.z
# stay ignored -- the C engine does not index those at runtime.
!cards/build/abilities_strings.bin

# Generated engine_c_wip sources
engine_c_wip/src/bytecode_data.c

# NOTE: `engine_c_wip/src/*.bin` below stays ignored on purpose. That glob
# covers src/abilities_strings.bin, which is a byte-for-byte COPY of the tracked
# pool above, made by the Makefile's `data` target so the test binaries can load
# it. The Makefile recreates it on every build, so it is never committed.
engine_c_wip/src/*.bin
engine_c_wip/src/core/generated/*.o
engine_c_wip/src/**/*.o

engine_c_wip/debug_eli.exe

# tools/decklog — this tool has two halves: scripts that GO GET deck data from
# decklog.bushiroad.com, and scripts that ANALYSE what was harvested. Only the
# analysis half is tracked (analyze_combos.py, analyze_meta.py, one_deck.py and
# README.md); the acquisition half and every artefact it produces are ignored.
# The split is by role, not by a file list — a new acquisition script or a new
# harvest output should be added below, never above.
#
# WHY ignore rather than commit: raw/ was 1000 verbatim API responses (20 MB) and
# sample/ another 302 files (10 MB) of other players' deck lists. The endpoint is
# anonymous, so a fresh scrape reproduces them exactly — they are not fixtures and
# nothing in the repo reads them at build or test time. Committing them would add
# ~30 MB of third-party deck lists to history for zero reproducibility gain, which
# is also what README.md "Notes" warns against redistributing.

# Acquisition half: the scripts that fetch the data. scrape_decklog.py downloads
# deck recipes (README "How it works"); export_codes_browser.js harvests the
# search-result deck codes from a logged-in browser session (README "Getting deck
# codes"), which is the only gated step and needs no credentials in-repo.
tools/decklog/scrape_decklog.py
tools/decklog/export_codes_browser.js

# Harvested data — regenerable by re-running the scraper.
tools/decklog/codes.txt
tools/decklog/decks.jsonl
tools/decklog/decks.txt
tools/decklog/decklog_import.txt
tools/decklog/search_meta.jsonl
tools/decklog/search_meta_partial_*.jsonl
tools/decklog/sample_codes.txt
tools/decklog/search_meta_sample.jsonl
tools/decklog/raw/
tools/decklog/sample/
# Per-deck HTML derived from raw/ by --html-dir. Same data as raw/, in the shape
# decklog's own view page renders, so web_ui/deck_converter.html can paste it.
tools/decklog/html/
# Session material and the dedicated Chrome profile used by login.py / harvest_all.py.
tools/decklog/.session.json
tools/decklog/.chrome-profile/

# Analysis OUTPUTS of analyze_combos.py, whose --out-dir defaults to
# tools/decklog/analysis. Tracked scripts, untracked results — same rule as the
# regenerable cards/ability_docs_scripts reports further up. Each run writes
# analysis_report.txt, combos.jsonl, deduped_decks.txt and unique_combos.txt;
# analysis_smoke/ is the same set from a reduced-input smoke run.
tools/decklog/analysis/
tools/decklog/analysis_smoke/
